Worked numbers, assuming an average weight size $m = 0.5$ and $\epsilon = 0.1$:

- a 2-feature model: the score moves by $0.1 \times 2 \times 0.5 = 0.1$;
- an 8x8 digit (64 pixels): by $3.2$;
- a 224 x 224 colour photo (150,528 numbers): by $7{,}526$.

The per-pixel change is the same in all three. Only the dimension differs. Deep networks are built from linear pieces (weighted sums and ReLU), so the same effect shows up there, which is why the paper's title speaks of "explaining" adversarial examples by linearity.
